WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:03.149
This is Germany in the 1980s.

00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:04.950
Before the Berlin Wall came down.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.470
Still fractured in two between two world superpowers,
and this is the map of Nuclear Power plants

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.370
that powered the country.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.449
The 1980s was an eventful time period for
this recovering nation.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:20.370
Chernobyl, located here, just north of Kiev,
exploded.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.750
Showering Europe in radioactive material.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.400
The severity of the incident can be hard to
grasp in hindsight.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.970
Iodine tablets were distributed across Europe.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.580
An emergency preventative medicine to ensure
that the thyroid gland is saturated in non-radioactive

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.039
iodine, preventing it from absorbing the radioactive
iodine present in nuclear fallout.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:43.160
Where it could fester and cause cancer.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.060
The fallout from Chernobyl spread on westerly
winds across Europe.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.079
And the news spread even faster.

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:50.730
The people of Europe were afraid.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:55.109
“Local people received 7.4 milligrays an
hour of radiation

00:00:55.109 --> 00:01:00.600
Radioactive cesium is recycling in the moss
Because I have been thinking all day about

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.210
this radiation radiation and nothing more
The chernobyl disaster could reap a bitter

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.650
harvest for the peasant farmers of Poland's
far north east

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:16.340
If the worst has already happened, the first
cases of leukemia should be appearing now.”

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.350
Chernobyl was given the highest ranking on
the International Nuclear Event Scale.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.909
A 7, a major accident with a large release
of radioactive materials.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.370
Or, in less technical terms, Europe was crop
dusted in cancer dust.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.370
[1]

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:36.140
This event, understandably, put a massive
dent in support for nuclear energy across

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.549
the world.

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:43.070
But, this wasn’t the genesis of the anti-nuclear
movement in Germany.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:49.860
In 1975, 30,000 protestors occupied the construction
site of a new nuclear power plant in south

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.840
western Germany, on the border with France.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:59.840
In 1979, 7 years before chernobyl, 200,000
protestors took to the streets of Germany

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.340
after the 3 Mile Island nuclear disaster in
the United States.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.470
The anti-nuclear movement in Germany was a
grassroots movement, led by the people, and

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.030
driven by fears of the very real danger nuclear
energy poses.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.319
There was a tremendous amount of political
pressure to begin phasing out these plants,

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:24.620
and these protests led directly to the creation
of Germany’s Green Party in 1980.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:31.300
The Chernobyl accident of 1986 simply strengthened
the support for the Greens political ideology.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.880
To phase out nuclear energy completely.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.271
In 1998, the Green Party came into power for
the first time, and the true end of nuclear

00:02:39.271 --> 00:02:41.840
power in Germany was all but secured.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.040
It was decided that no new nuclear power generators
would be built in the country from then forward,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.849
and in 2002 a law banning new nuclear energy
was passed.

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:57.629
With two nuclear power plants being taken
offline prematurely in Germany soon after.

00:02:57.629 --> 00:03:02.980
Angela Merkel, and her opposition party the
CDU, called these deactivations “destruction

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.610
of national property”

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:10.000
However, in the wake of the Fukushima Disaster,
even Merkel's opposition to the anti-nuclear

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.930
movement began to falter.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:17.209
Fukushima is the only other incident in the
history of nuclear power that was ranked on

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:22.450
par with Chernobyl, receiving the highest
ranking possible due to its impact on the

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:25.459
environment and the people of Japan.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:31.500
The Green Party’s power in Germany was now
at an all time high, winning control in influential

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.260
and powerful states in the south of Germany
for the first time.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.590
The anti-nuclear movement was too large a
political football, or perhaps nuclear football,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.989
to ignore, and Merkel’s CDU party joined
the anti-nuclear movement.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:50.260
Nuclear Energy had lost this war, and the
final nuclear power plants in Germany began

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.880
to flicker out.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:04:00.540
On April 15th 2023.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.260
The final nuclear power plant was shut down.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.620
Holding on through the energy crisis caused
by Russia’s invasion on Ukraine, it finally

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.580
met its planned end.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.970
The images of protestors, celebrating the
end of their 50 year war on nuclear energy,

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.950
were met with anger and disgust by pro-nuclear
activists online.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:26.620
Mirroring Merkal’s “destruction of national
property” outrage in the early 2000s.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:32.340
How could you celebrate something that so
clearly harms Germany’s goals of clean energy.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.500
Shutting its nuclear facilities has caused
Germany to be far more dependent on fossil

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.370
fuel than its neighbor France.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.919
This is what a typical day in France looks
like.

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:45.720
Baseload is provided by nuclear energy.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.260
Providing 65-75% of the country's power depending
on the time of day.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:56.600
Wind, Solar and Hydro play their part too,
with a small amount of flexible gas powered

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.470
plants available to quickly ramp up when called
upon.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:06.120
Releasing just 30-40 grams of carbon dioxide
per kilowatt hour generated.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.690
France is in a group of few.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.720
An industrial giant.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.160
Energy independent.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.230
Not reliant on foreign imports of fossil fuels.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.540
And not just powering its own country on low
carbon energy,

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:23.780
It also supports its own economy by exporting
massive quantities of this power to its neighbors

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.810
through high voltage interconnections.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:32.290
A rare example of a country with few fossil
fuel resources of its own becoming an energy

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.970
exporter.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.340
Germany couldn’t be any more different.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:42.210
Germany emits 440 grams of carbon dioxide
per kilowatt hour generated.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:48.680
Due to the fact that 20-25% of Germany’s
power comes from environmentally disastrous

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:49.680
coal.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.650
With a further 10-15% coming from gas.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.200
With the remaining power coming from wind,
solar and biomass.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.160
While Germany does receive a large percentage
of its power from renewables, without adequate

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.430
energy storage or a nuclear base load, the
country has become extremely dependent on

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:09.250
fossil fuels.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:13.930
This dependance became all the more apparent
as natural gas imports from Russia were cut

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:18.940
off, causing electricity prices to skyrocket
across Europe.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.039
This feels like a blatant own goal.

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:23.780
A 10 fold increase in carbon emissions.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:29.440
German’s aren’t ignorant of the damage
coal is having on their environment either.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.539
Expansions of coal mines have been protested
too, and the Green Party of Germany has been

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:38.380
under fire for its vote of approval for an
expansion of an open pit mine.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.169
If there has ever been an icon of industry
driven climate change it has to be the colossal

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:45.729
bucket excavators that roam this mine.

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:51.289
[2] The German government has expressed a
desire to phase out coal power soon, but their

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:57.389
only realistic option to do that currently
is to import even more natural gas.

00:06:57.389 --> 00:06:59.800
One thing needs to be acknowledged in this
debate.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.170
Both sides of this argument ultimately want
the same end goal.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.120
Clean, safe sustainable energy.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.180
We are on the same team.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:11.810
The disagreement is on what is considered
clean and safe.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:18.199
This exact argument went to the European Parliament
in 2022, where France fought for EU legislation

00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:22.720
to label Nuclear Energy and Natural Gas to
be labeled green.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.319
That second part will sound strange, but natural
gas does play a vital role in expanding renewables

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:31.569
in the absence of suitable energy storage.

00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:35.270
That subject needs an entire video to itself
to explain.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:42.650
This legislation divided the European Union,
with 328 votes for and 278 against.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:48.240
The success of the legislation angered many,
with politicians labeling it as “an odious

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.740
greenwashing attempt with Macron (the French
President) as conductor” [3]

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:57.159
Clearly this is a divisive issue, and GreenPeace
is currently trying to sue the EU over it,

00:07:57.159 --> 00:08:02.860
but this legislation paves the way for more
countries to emulate what France has done

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:08.599
and invest more money into nuclear energy
while meeting EU mandated sustainable energy

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:09.599
targets.

00:08:09.599 --> 00:08:10.599
[4].

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:14.410
France, on the surface, feels like the gold
standard of climate policy.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:19.970
[5] But, let’s take a deeper look at how
this came to be, and the challenges France

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.979
is facing in maintaining this energy policy.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:27.830
For that we need to rewind 50 years.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.610
It’s 1973.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:37.870
Egyptian and Syrian forces have launched a
surprise attack on Israel on the Jewish holiday

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:45.510
of Yom Kippur in an attempt to recapture land
on the Sinai peninsula, taken from Egypt in

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.440
1967 during the Six Day War.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.920
Western allies of Israel rushed to support
their counter offensives, and in retaliation

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:57.740
the union of Arab oil exporters embargoed
exports to these countries, causing oil prices

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.990
worldwide to skyrocket.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:05.019
Despite France not being a target of these
embargoes, it was a wakeup call for the powerful

00:09:05.019 --> 00:09:06.019
country.

00:09:06.019 --> 00:09:10.709
With few energy resources of its own, its
economy was extremely vulnerable to outside

00:09:10.709 --> 00:09:12.209
manipulation.

00:09:12.209 --> 00:09:18.250
Nuclear Energy was the answer, and from 1974
onwards nuclear energy capacity rapidly grew.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:19.650
cutscene of nuclear power plants coming online
and capacity rising.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:20.650
[6]

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:25.000
Over the course of these 23 years France increased
its nuclear capacity at an impressive rate

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:29.140
building 56 reactors in 19 different locations.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:34.660
However, new builds stopped abruptly in the
wake of Chernobyl, with only two new reactors

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.410
beginning construction in the aftermath of
this disaster.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:43.040
Civaux 1 and 2, taking 8 and 9 years to complete.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.870
They came online in 1997 and 1999.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.470
These are the youngest nuclear power plants
in France.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.570
24 and 26 years old.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:56.420
The oldest nuclear power plant currently is
the Bugey 2 reactor.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:01.200
Brought online in 1978, it is now 45 years
old.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:07.600
Its older sibling, Bugey 1 began operation
in 1972, and was decommissioned in 1994.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.710
It was an obsolete design, graphite moderated
and cooled with carbon dioxide.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:16.460
It needed to be decommissioned for safety
reasons.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:21.930
Nuclear power plants typically have a life
expectancy between 20 and 40 years, which

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.930
poses a problem to the French national grid,
and the European grid at large,

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:33.220
I listed every nuclear power reactor in France
on this excel sheet, and was honestly shocked

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:35.389
when I hit the averaging function.

00:10:35.389 --> 00:10:41.430
The average age of nuclear reactors in France
is now 39 years old, and these aging reactors

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.040
are beginning to cause some major headaches.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:50.540
In 2022, in the midst of an energy crisis
triggered by Russia’s invasion of Ukraine,

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:56.180
France’s energy utility provider, the EDF,
was ordered by the country's nuclear watchdog

00:10:56.180 --> 00:11:03.500
to inspect all plants after a 23 millimeter
deep crack was found in a 27 mm thick cooling

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:09.060
pipe which circulates low level radioactive
cooling water to the reactor.[7]The cracks

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:14.200
formed as a result of thermal fatigue in a
weld seam, where heating and cooling cycles

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.980
caused the pipe to expand and contract.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:21.510
This crack could have ruptured the pipe at
any moment, and its presence raised alarm

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:26.940
bells for the state of nuclear energy in the
country, and subsequent cracks were found

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.910
at other reactors.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:34.730
In the aftermath of these discoveries nuclear
energy output for the country fell to a 34

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:39.760
year low while these plants were taken offline
for inspection and repairs.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.660
This problem was a result of decades of under
investment and mismanagement of nuclear energy

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:50.830
in France, and to make matters worse, the
technical skills needed to fix the issue have

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:56.959
faded from existence in France as a result
of the 30 year gap in nuclear energy investments

00:11:56.959 --> 00:11:58.620
and construction.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:03.690
France is now rushing to invest and support
welding schools to bring back this important

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:04.690
skill.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.670
[8] And they need it desperately.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:12.440
Now, just as the Yom Kippur war woke France
up to its energy situation, the war in Ukraine

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:16.649
and these power outages are encouraging political
movement.

00:12:16.649 --> 00:12:20.690
France has begun a frantic reinvestment cycle
into nuclear energy.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:25.589
Just a few days after narrowly escaping an
ousting from Government through an unsuccessful

00:12:25.589 --> 00:12:31.560
no-confidence vote, a new nuclear energy investment
plan won a landslide victory.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:37.230
Gaining bipartisan support, the plan will
see 56 billion dollars be poured into building

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:41.149
6 next generation EPR2 nuclear reactors.

00:12:41.149 --> 00:12:42.149
[9]

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:47.490
The first generation EPR began construction
in north western France in 2007.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:49.339
Over 16 years ago.

00:12:49.339 --> 00:12:54.090
When the average age of French nuclear reactors
was just 21 years old.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:13:01.381
It still hasn’t delivered any power, and
is nearly 5 times over budget at 13.2 billion

00:13:01.381 --> 00:13:02.381
dollars.

00:13:02.381 --> 00:13:07.940
A far cry from the 3 billion dollars and 8
year construction time originally quoted.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:08.940
[10]

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:13.970
The second generation design has been specifically
redesigned to address these cost and construction

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:14.970
issues.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:19.070
The design challenge of making these nuclear
reactors safe, with all the knowledge we have

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:22.800
gained from accidents over the years, is immense.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.850
This is not an isolated incident.

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:33.339
This study states that out of 180 nuclear
construction projects, 175 of them overran

00:13:33.339 --> 00:13:35.029
cost estimations.

00:13:35.029 --> 00:13:42.449
Costing on average 117 percent more than estimated,
and taking 64 percent more time than projected.

00:13:42.449 --> 00:13:43.449
[11]

00:13:43.449 --> 00:13:48.639
The latest delay of this new reactor was due
to simple welding issues again.

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:53.720
When even your welds require specialized heat
treatments, requiring specialized skilled

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:58.170
laborers, and stringent safety checks, it’s
going to cause headaches.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:03.760
The reality is, Nuclear Energy keeps getting
more expensive as new safety standards are

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:07.100
realized with each accident that occurs.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.970
While renewable energy keeps getting cheaper.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:15.080
The first of the six next generation EPR2
reactors will not begin construction until

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:16.260
May 2027.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:22.009
Optimistically, they will take about 8 years
to construct at which point the average age

00:14:22.009 --> 00:14:27.000
of nuclear reactors in France will be 49 years
old.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.190
Aging nuclear reactors, to say the least,
are not ideal.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:36.730
Posing major reliability issues as we have
already seen, but a potentially disastrous

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:38.860
safety hazard too.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:44.079
France is staring down the barrel of major
electrical grid instabilities due to decades

00:14:44.079 --> 00:14:47.279
of under investment in nuclear energy.

00:14:47.279 --> 00:14:52.190
Nuclear energy, when done right, is clearly
the best solution to reduce carbon dioxide

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:58.000
emissions that we have right now, but the
problem is, it frequently isn’t done right.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.370
So, we have to ask ourselves.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:01.850
Does Germany have a point?

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.970
Were they right to close these nuclear reactors
down?

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:08.890
Or perhaps a middle ground between France
and Germany was needed.

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:15.060
Slowly ramping down nuclear energy while developing
new renewable energy resources with the energy

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.389
storage needed to create a stable grid

00:15:18.389 --> 00:15:23.610
Or perhaps we need to acknowledge the fact
that nuclear energy as it stands is not viable

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:29.449
long term, and we need to invest in future
technologies that make it safer, cleaner and

00:15:29.449 --> 00:15:30.449
cheaper.

00:15:30.449 --> 00:15:35.959
The reactor still under construction in France,
as it stands, has a cost of 8 million dollars

00:15:35.959 --> 00:15:37.509
per Megawatt.

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:42.650
While wind turbines cost between 1 and 2 million
dollars per megawatt, and critically, they

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:47.880
can be installed gradually without having
to commit billions to a single project.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.330
In a sense, wind turbines are modular.

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:53.399
Smaller, cheaper, easy to replace.

00:15:53.399 --> 00:15:59.279
One future tech being worked on right now
aims to give nuclear energy this same advantage.

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:03.899
Small modular nuclear reactors aim to make
smaller reactors that generate less power,

00:16:03.899 --> 00:16:08.640
but can have additional modules added over
time to increase capacity.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:13.970
Reducing initial capital investment, making
it easier to replace aging modules, and allowing

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:19.649
the reactors to be placed in locations not
suitable for larger traditional power plants.

00:16:19.649 --> 00:16:24.709
There are several companies working on these
designs now, and in the next episode of Real

00:16:24.709 --> 00:16:28.800
Engineering we deep dive into these technologies.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.779
Until then you may want to get ahead of the
game.

00:16:31.779 --> 00:16:36.420
I get asked by engineering students pretty
frequently if I have any advice for how to

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:37.699
do well in college.

00:16:37.699 --> 00:16:43.209
And I only really have two bits of advice
that I think is applicable to every person.

00:16:43.209 --> 00:16:47.949
First, get to know your teachers, they want
to help you and experienced and well connected

00:16:47.949 --> 00:16:51.290
friends are how you get opportunities in life.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.100
Second, get ahead of your peers.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:58.680
Students get a huge amount of time off in
the summer and most, including myself, wasted

00:16:58.680 --> 00:16:59.680
it.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:04.610
